Now, one more check on `sessionNamesQuick` — it calls `sessionNameQuick(metadata)` WITHOUT `hasRotatedSegments` — so it always lists segment paths per session via `_listSessionParts` (separator-safe). The `_lastListingParts` fast-path is only used when `hasRotatedSegments` is passed explicitly or `_lastListingParts` has the key... wait, in `sessionNameQuick`: `partsKnown = hasRotatedSegments ?? _lastListingParts?[metadata.path]` — inside `sessionNamesQuick` it does NOT pass the param, so `_lastListingParts` (if populated by the last listing AND not invalidated) decides. On POSIX, correct; on Windows, keys mismatch → null → full discovery. Perf-only degradation there. OK — my analysis stands.

Final check — the `.part-NN` regex change side effects: `_partSuffixPattern = RegExp(r'\.part-\d+$')`. Usages: only `_primaryOfPartName`? Let me grep to be sure there's no other consumer of `_partSuffixPattern` that relied on the `.jsonl` prefix. Earlier grep showed `_partSuffixPattern` only at 1396/1398 (declaration) + `_primaryOfPartName` at 1392. Only consumer. And `_primaryOfPartName` consumers: line 935 (new sweep walk) and line 137 (existing `_walkRoot`). Both want `foo.jsonl` now. Also `sessionPartPath`/`_parseSessionPartSeq` in session_storage use their own prefix matching (baseName + `.part-`), unaffected.

Could `\.part-\d+$` over-match? A session primary file itself never ends with `.part-N` (primaries end `.jsonl`). The walk only consults `_primaryOfPartName` for NON-`.jsonl` entries, so `weird.part-5` (a non-jsonl stray file) maps to primary `weird` — but `partPrimaries` only ever guards paths that ARE in `files` (`.jsonl` files), and `'$dirPath/weird'` won't equal any `.jsonl` path (they end in `.jsonl`)... wait: `partPrimaries.add('$dirPath/weird')` — the guard checks `partPrimaries.contains(path)` where path ends `.jsonl` — `weird` ≠ `foo.jsonl`. So stray matches are inert. In `_walkRoot` same inertness. OK, safe.

